# Every tracked text file is stored with LF and checked out with LF, on every
# platform and whatever a contributor's core.autocrlf happens to be. Without this
# the bytes that reach the repository depend on the machine they were written on,
# which produces diffs nobody made and, once a formatter check exists, a
# formatter that disagrees with itself between machines.
#
# text=auto lets git decide what is text; eol=lf fixes the direction for
# everything it decided was text, on the way in and on the way out.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Bytes rather than text. These are never converted in either direction, and
# marking them keeps them out of the text checks and out of diffs that would be
# unreadable anyway. Subject media is the reason most of this list exists: this
# project ingests plate images, and an image that git tried to normalise would be
# a corrupted image.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.tif binary
*.tiff binary
*.fits binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tar binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Exceptions to the normalisation above go here, by path, each carrying the
# reason it is excluded on the line above it. The case this list exists for is a
# fixture that proves behaviour on a particular byte sequence: normalising it
# would silently delete the byte the fixture is about, the test would then pass
# for the wrong reason, and nothing would say so. Such a path is declared -text
# and never left to text=auto.
#
# There is no such path in the tree today. This paragraph is the whole of the
# list, and it is written out rather than left empty so the next person adding a
# fixture finds the rule at the place they would break it.
